ID: B2-Ch02a-Intro
TEXT:

"Listen, Arraka," Daren says as we make our way toward the arena. "Are you certain the Eiden you fought during the still winter is the same man we saw back there? Six centuries have passed. No one would fault you for confusing two faces after that kind of time."

"This is the third time you have asked me that exact question," Arraka snaps from the amulet. "Yes, it is the same Eiden. Hammer it through that thick skull of yours and leave it there."

"Why is that so hard to accept, Daren?" Kate asks. "We already knew he was six hundred years old, and that he ran in the same circles as Arraka and the golden fox. Given the power he threw around in the courtyard, it is hardly a leap to believe he was the one who brought her down."

"His strength is not what bothers me," Daren says. "What bothers me is that he is revered as the founder of civilization on this continent, yet everything we have seen him do amounts to mindless chaos for his own amusement."

"Six centuries change people," Kate says. "Didn't he tell you at the start that he keeps his eyes shut because he grew tired of looking at human ugliness? You saw him with the earl and the ambassador from Ollendor. He has nothing left for the people of Varathia except contempt."

"Leaving Eiden aside," I say, "shouldn't we be planning for the arena?"

"Nothing to fret over," Hadrik says, grinning. "I can handle whatever the ambassador throws into the ring on my own."

"And if they separate us?" I ask.

"Barry has the right of it," Daren says. "The priority is keeping our lines open even if they lock us in different cells."

He shrugs his pack off one shoulder and pulls three transceivers from the top flap.

"Kate, Hadrik, Flower, take these," he says, handing one to each of them. "Barry, you already have yours. There is a dial on the right casing to adjust your transmission frequency to match another device. Everyone see it?"

We murmur our agreement.

"Good," Daren says. "Each unit carries a number corresponding to its unique signature frequency. Memorize the others so you know whose line you are calling. The numbers are stamped into the back plates."

I flip mine over. A clean numeral two is pressed into the brass. We trade numbers down the line: Kate is three, Flower is four, and Hadrik is five.

"There you have it," Daren says. "If you need to reach an unenchanted transceiver, the toggle beside the dial drops you back to standard frequencies. I will be on number six."

"What about number one?" I ask.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Intro2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
Daren goes quiet for a long moment.

"That was my original unit," he says softly. "The one I gave to Rose."

The name hangs over us, and no one speaks.

"So," Hadrik says, breaking the heavy silence, "are we sure the guards will let us keep these once we are behind bars?"

"You may retain your personal effects," the royal servant says, glancing back as he guides us down the street. "Each participant is assigned an individual cell for storage, and you are permitted to keep small items on your person."

"What about arms?" Daren asks. "Are we allowed our own weapons and armor in the ring?"

"Certainly, provided they do not channel spells," the servant replies.

"What falls under spellcasting?" I ask. "Would an enchantment that boosts physical capabilities be barred?"

"That depends entirely on the mechanism," the servant says. "Without inspecting the object in question, I cannot offer a ruling."

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
