"Never mind, then," I say.

"Barry," Daren cuts in, drawing our attention.

"How tightly are we pressed for time?" Daren asks the servant. "Can we spare half an hour? I would like to lay basic edge and durability enchantments on my companions' daggers, assuming there is a smith selling elven steel along our path."

"We have time," the servant says. "The market ahead has several purveyors of enchanting stock."

"Why elven steel specifically?" I ask Daren.

"It is the finest grade coin can buy," Daren says. "Foundation enchantments like sharpness and resilience depend almost entirely on the purity of the metal receiving the spell."

"Any chance you could reinforce my crossbow while you are at it?" I ask.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Ranged weapons are prohibited inside Varathian arenas," the servant says.

"To be frank, Barry," Daren says, "even if they allowed it, I would advise against enchanting that crossbow. You would be better served buying a proper stock. A basic spell cannot compensate for flawed woodwork. Blades are different; the quality of the raw ore matters far more than the smith's initial fold."

"Why harp on basic work?" Hadrik asks. "You are supposed to be a master enchanter. Why not lay on some of the wild spells you have running along your broadsword?"

Daren scowls at him.

"Advanced enchantments demand weeks of preparation and costly catalysts," he says. "The work on my blade took a fortune in reagents. For the duration of this tournament, basic reinforcement is the extent of what I can deliver. Barry, Kate, hand over your daggers. I will treat them both the moment we reach that stall."

When we reach the market stall, Daren ducks inside and emerges thirty minutes later on the dot.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Ranged weapons are prohibited inside Varathian arenas," the servant says.

"To be frank, Barry," Daren says, "even if they allowed it, I would advise against enchanting that crossbow. You would be better served buying a proper stock. A basic spell cannot compensate for flawed woodwork. Blades are different; the quality of the raw ore matters far more than the smith's initial fold."

"Why harp on basic work?" Hadrik asks. "You are supposed to be a master enchanter. Why not lay on some of the wild spells you have running along your broadsword?"

Daren scowls at him.

"Advanced enchantments demand weeks of preparation and costly catalysts," he says. "The work on my blade took a fortune in reagents. For the duration of this tournament, basic reinforcement is the extent of what I can deliver. Barry, Kate, hand over your daggers. I will treat them both the moment we reach that stall."

When we reach the market stall, Daren ducks inside and emerges thirty minutes later on the dot.

While he works, I use my transceiver to contact Rose and let her know we will miss dinner tonight. Alarm laces her voice at first, but once I lay out the terms, relief takes over at the prospect of a path to freedom. She promises she will be in the arena stands, cheering when our match begins.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Gems, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Gems)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Gems
TEXT:

#if(v_ch10_gems == 1) {
Daren steps out of the shop, hands Kate her newly balanced blade, and turns to me with an open palm held flat at waist height.

"Ten gold coins," Daren says. "Western continent mint is fine if that is what you have."

"You are charging me?" I ask.

"What did you expect?" Daren says. "Elven steel does not come as a gift."

"You didn't ask Kate for a copper!"

"Kate was not seen stuffing her pockets with uncut gems in an ogre fortress three days ago," Daren says evenly. "You, on the other hand, have a purse that can bear it."

I grin. "Caught in the act."

"Cough it up," he says, hand still out.

"Fine, fine," I mutter, counting ten heavy coins into his palm before reclaiming my dagger.

The trek from the bazaar to the arena district takes another two hours through tightening streets. We part ways with the royal servant outside a squat, stone-faced annex marked as the administrative intake. Inside, the place feels less like an office and more like a guardhouse sally port. A lone clerk sits behind a heavy timber desk, sorting parchment into neat stacks. He finishes aligning his ledger before looking up, greeting us with a dry nod.

"I received word to expect your party tonight," he says. "Three fighters for the sands, correct?"

"Four," Hadrik says, grinning broadly as he slaps the king's sealed writ onto the desk.

"I see," the clerk says, breaking the seal and skimming the lines. "Very well. As the sole intake officer on duty at this hour, you will remain in the anteroom while I escort each of you to your quarters individually."

}
#if(v_ch10_gems == 0) {
"Wait, aren't you going to charge us for the work?" Kate asks Daren once he hands both blades back.

"No need," Daren says. "The steel was reasonably priced, and my purse is in no danger."

Kate eyes him with clear skepticism, but lets the matter rest.

The trek from the bazaar to the arena district takes another two hours through tightening streets. We part ways with the royal servant outside a squat, stone-faced annex marked as the administrative intake. Inside, the place feels less like an office and more like a guardhouse sally port. A lone clerk sits behind a heavy timber desk, sorting parchment into neat stacks. He finishes aligning his ledger before looking up, greeting us with a dry nod.

"I received word to expect your party tonight," he says. "Three fighters for the sands, correct?"

"Four," Hadrik says, grinning broadly as he slaps the king's sealed writ onto the desk.

"I see," the clerk says, breaking the seal and skimming the lines. "Very well. As the sole intake officer on duty at this hour, you will remain in the anteroom while I escort each of you to your quarters individually."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Cell, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Cell)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Cell
TEXT:

"Can't we head down as a group?" Hadrik asks.

"Out of the question," the clerk says. "The ambassador of Ollendor gave explicit instructions that none of you are to know the location of the others' cells. You are assigned to separate divisions to prevent collusion during the trials."

"Divisions?" Daren asks, brow furrowing.

"We begin with the lessathi," the clerk says, ignoring him entirely.

He unlatches an iron-reinforced door in the back wall and gestures to the dark beyond.

"This way."

I trade a quick look with the rest of the group and step through the frame.

"Good luck, Barry!" Flower calls after me. "Petal and I will be shouting for you from the front row! Arraka too!"

The heavy door thuds shut behind me, cutting off the lobby. I fall in behind the clerk as we descend a spiral flight of worn granite steps deep into the foundations. We walk in silence through damp, unmortared tunnels lit only by sputtering pitch torches. Corridors branch and double back in identical stone arcs until every sense of direction unravels. 

After several minutes of steady descent, the clerk speaks without looking back.

"Two vacancies remain in your division," he says. "Cell three and cell five, on opposite flanks of the yard. I have no specific placement orders. Which do you take?"

"What is the difference between them?" I ask.

"None," the clerk says flatly.

"None at all?"

"None whatsoever."

"Then why ask?"

"Because a choice must be recorded. Three or five?"

choice("Choose cell number three", B2-Ch02a-Bulky, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Bulky, v_b2_ch2_cell = 1)
choice("Choose cell number five", B2-Ch02a-Bulky, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Bulky, v_b2_ch2_cell = 2)
choice("Tell him that I don't care either way", B2-Ch02a-Bulky, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Bulky, v_b2_ch2_cell = 3)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Bulky
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_cell == 1) {
"Cell three, then."

"Very well," the clerk says. "Cell three sits along the left wall of the common yard. The numeral is carved into the lintel."

He halts before a heavy oak door and draws an iron key from his tunic.

"This is your cell key," he says, pressing the cold iron into my hand. "Secure your effects inside whenever you leave. You may keep to your quarters as much as you like, though division regulations require at least two hours daily in the yard during recreation periods to foster coordination with your squad."

He works a second key into the main lock and swings the passage open.

"This leads directly to your division's common area. I advise sleep. Muster begins early tomorrow."

I nod and cross the threshold.

"Farewell," the clerk says, and the door slams shut behind me with a heavy click.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_cell == 2) {
"Cell five, then."

"Very well," the clerk says. "Cell five sits along the right wall of the common yard. The numeral is carved into the lintel."

He halts before a heavy oak door and draws an iron key from his tunic.

"This is your cell key," he says, pressing the cold iron into my hand. "Secure your effects inside whenever you leave. You may keep to your quarters as much as you like, though division regulations require at least two hours daily in the yard during recreation periods to foster coordination with your squad."

He works a second key into the main lock and swings the passage open.

"This leads directly to your division's common area. I advise sleep. Muster begins early tomorrow."

I nod and cross the threshold.

"Farewell," the clerk says, and the door slams shut behind me with a heavy click.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_cell == 3) {
"Makes no difference to me," I say. "Pick one."

"Cell five it is," the clerk says in the same flat drone. "Your quarters lie along the right wall of the common yard. The numeral is carved into the lintel."

He halts before a heavy oak door and draws an iron key from his tunic.

"This is your cell key," he says, pressing the cold iron into my hand. "Secure your effects inside whenever you leave. You may keep to your quarters as much as you like, though division regulations require at least two hours daily in the yard during recreation periods to foster coordination with your squad."

He works a second key into the main lock and swings the passage open.

"This leads directly to your division's common area. I advise sleep. Muster begins early tomorrow."

I nod and cross the threshold.

"Farewell," the clerk says, and the door slams shut behind me with a heavy click.

}
A few guttering tallow candles provide the only light in the cavernous common room. The space is mostly empty, save for a broad-shouldered man in scarred leather armor who pushes off a pillar and stalks straight toward me.

"I've been waiting for you," he rumbles, voice thick with threat. "Word came down they were dragging you in tonight."

"Do I know you?" I ask.

"No reason you should," he says, halting an arm's reach away. "I don't know your face either."

"Then why wait up?"

"Guards talk," the man says. "They were whispering about a band of mages that slaughtered the earl of Ollendor, and said one of the killers was getting dumped in our block. I have to admit, I didn't expect a stillwater."

I hold my tongue. No reason to correct him; letting the block assume I am a seasoned stillwater is far safer than advertising that I have no magic at all.

"Not that it matters in here," he continues, leaning in. "You could be one of the Four Sages for all the good it does you. Spells are forbidden. I could cave your skull in with my bare hands and you couldn't conjure a spark to stop me."

"Why would you want to?" I ask. "Aren't we supposed to fight on the same side?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Gallows, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Gallows)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Gallows
TEXT:

His face twists into a snarl.

"Do you have any idea what you've done, you arrogant bastard?" he roars.

"Not a clue," I say. "Enlighten me."

"I'll enlighten you," he spits, teeth grinding. "Your little stunt with that noble signed death warrants for every man in this division. The guards are saying the coming match will be the bloodiest in the history of Varathia. They aren't expecting a single survivor. They are feeding all of us to the slaughter just to guarantee you die in the dirt."

His fists clench so violently that blood wells where his nails bite into his palms. Across the yard, cell doors creak open as the shout echoes through the vaults, silhouettes stepping into the candlelight to watch the confrontation.

"The only reason I signed on in Thilias," the man says, "was because the gnome king keeps his sands clean. Gladiators rarely die on his floor. An easy purse, I thought. And now? Now I am sitting on death row, waiting for the butcher's axe. Cursing my luck. Cursing you!"

"Why not simply walk away?" I say.

"You think I haven't tried?" he barks, voice bouncing off the stone rafters. "The arena master has the gates barred. It is the first time in a decade the crown has granted him permission to make the spectacle as lethal as he wants. He has a private menagerie of rare horrors chained in the under-tunnels, starved and waiting for a crowd. Now that he has his permit, he plans to loose every single beast on us in one massive bloodbath. His masterwork. He doesn't care what the king does to him afterward so long as the show brings down the house. He is ready to burn, and he is dragging us with him."

"Pull yourself together," I tell him. "You are talking like a man on the gallows, not a fighter facing a match. I didn't come here to drown in self-pity. I came to win."

That strips away whatever fragile restraint he had left. His right fist flies straight at my face with full force.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Fist, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Fist)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Fist
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch2_bro,1) if (v_toughness >= 2)
set(v_b2_ch2_fist,1) if (v_toughness >= 2)
set(v_b2_ch2_fist,2) if (v_strength >= 2 && v_toughness < 2)
set(v_b2_ch2_fist,3) if (v_combat_technique >= 2 && v_strength < 2 && v_toughness < 2)
set(v_b2_ch2_fist,4) if (v_reflexes >= 2 && v_combat_technique < 2 && v_strength < 2 && v_toughness < 2)
set(v_b2_ch2_fist,5) if (v_combat_technique < 2 && v_reflexes < 2 && v_strength < 2 && v_toughness < 2)
achievement("Come at me, bro!",v_ac_b2_ch2_bro)
#if(v_toughness >= 2) {
I make no move to evade. His knuckles slam into the bridge of my nose with a sharp crack that barely registers as pressure. My head doesn't even tilt. As he pulls his hand back, shaking out stinging fingers, I meet his glare with flat, indifferent boredom.

Fury floods his face. He steps into a heavy combination, driving piston strikes into my jaw, ribs, and solar plexus for twenty breathless seconds. I stand there with my arms loose, taking every impact without shifting a foot. He stumbles back on trembling legs, chest heaving, staring at me in open disbelief.

"Are you finished?" I ask.

"Maybe I had you wrong, stillwater," he wheezes, rubbing his battered knuckles.

He steps in and clamps both heavy hands onto my shoulders, eyes suddenly alight with desperate hope.

"Maybe you're right! Maybe we can survive this... if we stand together!"

}
#if(v_strength >= 2 && v_toughness < 2) {
I swat his incoming wrist aside with my left forearm and drive a stiff right directly into his solar plexus before he can recover. The blow blows every drop of air from his lungs, dropping him hard to his knees. He stays there on all fours for ten seconds, gasping at the flagstones, before staggering back to his feet. Disbelief replaces his scowl.

"Well?" I say. "Care to try again?"

"Maybe I had you wrong, stillwater," he mutters, shaking his head slowly.

He steps in and clamps both heavy hands onto my shoulders, eyes suddenly alight with desperate hope.

"Maybe you're right! Maybe we can survive this... if we stand together!"

}
#if(v_combat_technique >= 2 && v_strength < 2 && v_toughness < 2) {
I slip inside his reach, snare his wrist, pivot, and wrench his arm up into a tight hammerlock against his spine. The leverage drops him to one knee, a sharp hiss escaping through his teeth as I crank the joint toward its breaking point.

"Do you yield?" I ask into his ear.

He grits his teeth and strains against the lock, muscles straining against bone.

"Do you yield?" I demand, wrenching the elbow higher.

The pressure breaks his pride in a ragged shout.

"Yes! Hell take you, I yield! I yield!"

I release the hold. He scrambles back several paces, rotating his sore shoulder while sweat beads across his brow.

"Maybe I underestimated you, stillwater," he mutters. "You can handle yourself without magic. But brawling tricks won't be enough to walk out of that arena alive."

"Don't worry," I say. "I have plenty more in reserve."

"We'll see," he grunts, turning on his heel and heading for his quarters.

Across the yard, two onlookers retreat into their cells. One man pushes off the wall and walks toward me—roughly my age, wearing his dark hair in a tidy ponytail and carrying a smooth, polite smile that uncomfortably echoes Eiden's. The fourth spectator remains framed in her doorway: a frail girl with long silver hair, looking barely twenty, watching me with steady intensity.

The man with the ponytail halts a polite distance away and inclines his head.

"Holding your own against Bruce without spellcraft is impressive," he says smoothly. "Few fighters step into his reach like that. May I have your name?"

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 2 && v_combat_technique < 2 && v_strength < 2 && v_toughness < 2) {
I sway back on my heels, the knuckle sweep passing a fraction of an inch from my chin. Bruce follows with a heavy left hook; my footing slips slightly on the smooth flags, but I roll under the arc with room to spare. For thirty seconds he unleashes a relentless flurry of hooks and crosses, attempting to trap me against a pillar. I weave through the storm, pivoting and ducking inside the narrowest margins, keeping just beyond his reach.

His arms finally drop, chest working hard as he glares at me.

"Now I see where the confidence comes from," he spits. "You can dance. But don't think you can evade forever. The match ends only when the beasts fall or we do."

"Dodging is hardly my only skill," I say.

"We'll see," he grunts, turning on his heel and heading for his quarters.

Across the yard, two onlookers retreat into their cells. One man pushes off the wall and walks toward me—roughly my age, wearing his dark hair in a tidy ponytail and carrying a smooth, polite smile that uncomfortably echoes Eiden's. The fourth spectator remains framed in her doorway: a frail girl with long silver hair, looking barely twenty, watching me with steady intensity.

The man with the ponytail halts a polite distance away and inclines his head.

"Holding your own against Bruce without spellcraft is impressive," he says smoothly. "Few fighters step into his reach like that. May I have your name?"

}
#if(v_combat_technique < 2 && v_reflexes < 2 && v_strength < 2 && v_toughness < 2) {
I try to duck, but my feet lag behind my eyes. The fist catches me square across the jaw, sending me crashing hard against the flagstones. The vaulted ceiling spins in sickening circles. By the time I drag myself upright, another inmate has Bruce locked in a rear choke, hauling the big man backward.

"Settle yourself, Bruce," the newcomer says evenly. "We need every warm body we can get before the gate lifts."

"Get off me," Bruce snarls, wrenching free with a violent twist.

He glares down at me with pure disdain.

"If a simple cross puts you on the floor," he spits, "there is no need for me to dirty my hands. The beasts will tear you apart in the opening minutes. We'll see how sharp your tongue is then."

He turns and stomps toward his quarters. Looking at the man who stepped in, I find someone roughly my age with his dark hair drawn into a neat ponytail, offering a calm, courteous smile that carries an uncomfortable trace of Eiden's detachment.

"Pay him no mind," he says, gesturing toward the retreating brute. "Bruce is rattled by the sudden change of the hour. He will settle by morning. May I have your name?"

}
choice(""Well, we are part of the same team."", B2-Ch02a-Team, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Team) if (v_strength >= 2 || v_toughness >= 2)
choice(""How about you apologize for attacking me, first?"", B2-Ch02a-Apologize, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Apologize) if (v_strength >= 2 || v_toughness >= 2)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Unusual, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Unusual) if (v_strength < 2 && v_toughness < 2)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Team
TEXT:

"Well, we are part of the same team."

"That we are!" he barks, a wide grin splitting his rough features as he claps a heavy palm onto my shoulder in genuine respect.

With that, Bruce turns and lumbers back to his cell.

Across the common room, two onlookers slip into their quarters and pull the doors shut. One man pushes off a pillar and walks toward me—roughly my age, wearing his dark hair in a neat ponytail and carrying a polite, measured smile that reminds me of Eiden. The final spectator lingers by her door: a frail girl with long silver hair, watching me with unblinking focus.

The man with the ponytail halts a pace away and bows his head slightly.

"Holding your own against Bruce without spellcraft is impressive," he says. "Not many would step into his reach like that. May I ask your name?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Unusual, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Unusual)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Apologize
TEXT:

"How about you apologize for attacking me, first?"

A booming laugh breaks from his chest as he drops his hands.

"If an apology is what you want, stillwater, take it!" he says. "I don't shy from my own blunders. I assumed a mage was soft prey, and I swung without cause. Now, will you stand beside me on the sands?"

"I suppose so," I say. "We are on the same side, after all."

"That we are!" he says, grinning broadly and nodding with heavy respect.

With that, Bruce turns and lumbers back to his cell.

Across the common room, two onlookers slip into their quarters and pull the doors shut. One man pushes off a pillar and walks toward me—roughly my age, wearing his dark hair in a neat ponytail and carrying a polite, measured smile that reminds me of Eiden. The final spectator lingers by her door: a frail girl with long silver hair, watching me with unblinking focus.

The man with the ponytail halts a pace away and bows his head slightly.

"Holding your own against Bruce without spellcraft is impressive," he says. "Not many would step into his reach like that. May I ask your name?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Unusual, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Unusual)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Unusual
TEXT:

#if(v_premonition < 2) {
"Barry."

"Barry?" he repeats, eyebrow rising. "An unusual name for a mage."

"So I've been told."

"Forgive me, I meant no slight. It is simply that most casters I meet carry names designed to impress. The moment families discover a magical spark in their bloodline, they feel compelled to parade it with titles like Zalnir or Vasiroth. An unspoken tradition. But to settle on something as ordinary as Barry..."

"My parents never cared much for tradition."

"I see," he says, extending a hand with an easy smile. "I am Wilhelm. Well met."

I clasp his hand, and his grip is steady and warm.

"Look," Wilhelm says, "I know the welcome here was rough, but bad blood among teammates before a trial like this is the quickest path to a shallow grave. I wanted you to know you have an ally here."

}
#if(v_premonition >= 2) {
"Barry."

"Barry?" he repeats, eyebrow rising. "An unusual name for a mage."

"So I've been told."

"Forgive me, I meant no slight. It is simply that most casters I meet carry names designed to impress. The moment families discover a magical spark in their bloodline, they feel compelled to parade it with titles like Zalnir or Vasiroth. An unspoken tradition. But to settle on something as ordinary as Barry..."

"My parents never cared much for tradition."

"I see," he says, extending a hand with an easy smile. "I am Wilhelm. Well met."

As our hands meet, a jagged premonition rips through my vision: the sands of an arena, and Wilhelm driving an orange-hilted dagger between my shoulder blades from behind.

"Is something wrong?" Wilhelm asks, tracking the shift in my posture.

"Nothing," I say smoothly, locking down the reaction. "Just tired."

"Look," Wilhelm continues, "I know the welcome here was rough, but bad blood among teammates before a trial like this is the quickest path to a shallow grave. I wanted you to know you have an ally here."

}
choice(""Thanks for letting me know."", B2-Ch02a-Thanks, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Thanks, v_b2_ch2_threatened = 0) if (v_premonition < 2)
choice(""Sorry, but I prefer to work alone."", B2-Ch02a-Sorry, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Sorry, v_b2_ch2_threatened = 0) if (v_premonition < 2)
choice("Act casually, without letting him know that I'm on to him.", B2-Ch02a-Premonition, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Premonition, v_b2_ch2_threatened = 1) if (v_premonition >= 2)
choice("Threaten him, in order to make sure that he'll be too scared to stab me in the back.", B2-Ch02a-Premonition, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Premonition, v_b2_ch2_threatened = 2) if (v_premonition >= 2)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Thanks
TEXT:

"Thanks for letting me know," I say.

"That is all I wanted to say tonight," Wilhelm says. "We should both get some rest. Recreation period begins early tomorrow."

"I heard. Good night."

"To you as well," Wilhelm says, turning toward his cell.

Across the room, the girl with the silver hair holds my gaze for a few beats longer before slipping into her room and pulling the door shut. With nothing left to do in the common area, I head for my own cell. Inside, the sole piece of furniture is a broad stone slab built into the masonry. I turn the heavy iron key in the lock, drop my pack against the base of the wall, and sit down on the slab.

A narrow ventilation slit near the ceiling admits a sliver of candlelight from the outer patrol tunnel, but no footsteps disturb the corridor. The guards are either asleep or watching another wing. I pull a spare sweater from my pack to cushion the cold stone and lay my head down. Sleep comes fast, lasting until a harsh voice rattles the iron door in the morning.

"Muster!" the guard barks from the corridor. "Recreation period is open. Out into the yard, move it!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Recreation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Recreation)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Sorry
TEXT:

"Sorry, but I prefer to work alone."

"Understood," Wilhelm says smoothly. "The last thing I want is to crowd your style. But if you find yourself in a tight spot during the match, remember you have at least one hand to call upon."

He pauses, then steps back.

"That is all I wanted to say tonight," he adds. "We should both get some rest. Recreation period begins early tomorrow."

"I heard. Good night."

"To you as well," Wilhelm says, turning toward his cell.

Across the room, the girl with the silver hair holds my gaze for a few beats longer before slipping into her room and pulling the door shut. With nothing left to do in the common area, I head for my own cell. Inside, the sole piece of furniture is a broad stone slab built into the masonry. I turn the heavy iron key in the lock, drop my pack against the base of the wall, and sit down on the slab.

A narrow ventilation slit near the ceiling admits a sliver of candlelight from the outer patrol tunnel, but no footsteps disturb the corridor. The guards are either asleep or watching another wing. I pull a spare sweater from my pack to cushion the cold stone and lay my head down. Sleep comes fast, lasting until a harsh voice rattles the iron door in the morning.

"Muster!" the guard barks from the corridor. "Recreation period is open. Out into the yard, move it!"

