"I am offering you a simple choice," I say. "I don't know who bought your contract or what grudge you carry. But you are an assassin, not a frontline duelist. You survive on ambush and blind spots. You can still try for my spine, but the problem is that I am already waiting for the strike. If you think the bounty is worth that gamble, step up. If not, stay across the yard."

"Understood," Wilhelm says, the color draining from his face. "I will not trouble you."

He turns and retreats to his corner.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Princess, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Princess)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Princess
TEXT:

Across the yard, the silver-haired girl is still watching me. She hasn't shifted her stance once. Wilhelm catches the direction of my gaze and offers a faint smirk.

"I wouldn't waste breath on that one," Wilhelm murmurs. "Every man here has tried. Our little silver-haired princess is either mute or bound by a vow of silence. You won't draw a syllable."

He bows his head and walks back to his wall. As he passes her, she ignores him entirely, keeping her eyes fixed on mine.

She is doing it deliberately. What does she want? An invitation? There is only one way to find out.

I cross the flags toward her, tracking her posture. She doesn't tense or look away, holding my gaze as I halt two paces back.

Close up, she stands a full head shorter than me, slender and frail-looking under a plain beige linen blouse and short leggings cut for agility. Her wooden sandals click softly as she adjusts her weight. Despite the delicate frame, twin fighting knives hang from her belt, and dark purple bruises mottle the skin of her bare forearms and shins—the marks of a vicious brawl shortly before she landed in the cells.

She offers no greeting, so I take the lead.

"Are you going to tell me why you've been staring at me for five straight minutes?"

Her expression does not shift.

"Nothing?" I ask. "Not a word?"

Still silence. Wilhelm called it: a waste of time.

I pivot to walk away, but fingers snare my sleeve.

I glance back. Her small hand grips my cuff, and glowing blue letters hang suspended in the air between us, formed of crisp light in Common script:

Wait.

The letters dissolve like smoke, instantly replaced by a fresh line:

Is it true, what they say about you?

"What do they say?" I ask.

The glowing script reforms:

They say you were brought here because you attacked a noble. Is that true?

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Princess2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Princess2)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Princess2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
"That covers the main thrust of it," I say.

The blue letters shift:

But why attack a noble? Didn't you know how dangerous it would be to strike a noble in Varathia, let alone kill one?

"We knew," I say. "We were freeing a slave. Besides, the bastard had it coming."

The light fades, pausing for a few seconds before the next sentence glimmers into being:

You don't seem to hold our nobility in high regard.

"I hate them with a passion," I tell her.

A small, genuine smile breaks across her face, softening the tight lines of her jaw.

The blue script glimmers again:

I see. Please, allow me to introduce myself. My name is Leila, and I was brought here for freeing a slave from a noble as well.

She extends a bruised hand toward me.

"I'm Barry," I say, clasping her fingers. "Well met."

Her free hand twitches slightly, and the blue text writes across the empty air:

Barry, I have a favor to ask. Could we continue our conversation inside my cell? There is something else I want to ask you, and I would rather do it in private.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
"Technically a companion of mine struck the killing blow, not me," I say, "though I backed him without reservation."

The blue letters shift:

But why attack a noble? Didn't you know how dangerous it would be to strike a noble in Varathia, let alone kill one?

"We knew," I say. "We were freeing a slave. Besides, the bastard had it coming."

The light fades, pausing for a few seconds before the next sentence glimmers into being:

You don't seem to hold our nobility in high regard.

"I hate them with a passion," I tell her.

A small, genuine smile breaks across her face, softening the tight lines of her jaw.

The blue script glimmers again:

I see. Please, allow me to introduce myself. My name is Leila, and I was brought here for freeing a slave from a noble as well.

She extends a bruised hand toward me.

"I'm Barry," I say, clasping her fingers. "Well met."

Her free hand twitches slightly, and the blue text writes across the empty air:

Barry, I have a favor to ask. Could we continue our conversation inside my cell? There is something else I want to ask you, and I would rather do it in private.

}
choice("Accept to go inside her cell.", B2-Ch02a-Private, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Private, v_b2_ch2_lessathi = 0)
choice("Do not accept to go inside her cell.", B2-Ch02a-Private, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Private, v_b2_ch2_lessathi = 1)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Private
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_lessathi == 0) {
"All right, let's go," I say.

Leila nods and unlatches the door. As we cross the threshold into her cell, Wilhelm watches from his corner, quiet disappointment tightening the corners of his mouth when I accept her invitation so readily.

The moment the iron bolt clacks home, my instincts scream, and I leap back against the far wall, hands coming up.

Leila blinks, tilting her head. The glowing blue letters reform in midair:

What are you doing?

"Checking for an ambush," I tell her.

Her eyebrows knit together, text hanging between us:

An ambush? For what purpose? Striking or maiming a member of your own division is strictly forbidden.

"No one handed me a copy of the rules on the way down," I say.

That is standard across every arena in Varathia!

"I'm not from around here, remember?"

She lets out a soundless sigh and perches on the edge of her stone slab.

In that case, take care not to harm the organizers or spectators either. The penalty is identical.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_lessathi == 1) {
"If you have questions, ask them here," I say.

Leila does not move or write for several seconds, her steady gaze holding mine.

Very well, the glowing text writes at last. Then I will ask you here.

A brief pause, then fresh script illuminates the air:

You're not really a stillwater, are you, Barry?

A prickle of cold runs down my spine. Across the yard, Wilhelm has drifted a few paces closer, watching us with keen interest.

"Of course I am a stillwater," I tell her smoothly. "What makes you think otherwise?"

The fact that you are a lessathi.

"Fine, message received," I mutter. "Inside."

A faint smile touches her lips. She turns the key and pulls the door open. Wilhelm tracks our retreat, a small smirk playing on his face.

"You don't take no for an answer, do you?" I ask as the bolt clicks into place.

I am sorry, she writes. But you invited the topic. Why refuse to step inside?

"Because I met you five minutes ago," I say. "For all I knew, you were leading me into a trap. I'm still not entirely convinced you aren't."

She looks at me with genuine surprise.

A trap? What could I possibly gain? Maiming or killing a member of your own division is against the rules.

"No one handed me a copy of the rules on the way down," I say.

That is standard across every arena in Varathia!

"I'm not from around here, remember?"

She lets out a soundless sigh and perches on the edge of her stone slab.

In that case, take care not to harm the organizers or spectators either. The penalty is identical.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Outcasts, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Outcasts)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Outcasts
TEXT:

"Are there any other standard regulations I should know about?" I ask.

Yes, Leila writes. Active spells and ranged weapons are barred from the ring, as are artifacts that cast spells. But rest easy: your stat booster is exempt. The arena's detection wards will pass over it without reacting.

My jaw drops, and she breaks into a silent laugh, one hand coming up to cover her mouth.

Yes, I know about your stat device, she writes, amusement dancing in her eyes.

"How?"

She dips two fingers into her tunic pocket and draws out a polished metal device, identical in size and shape to my own.

My stat booster carries custom modifications, the blue script explains. Beyond translating my thoughts into visible words, it detects active stat devices nearby. My father built the mechanism. When you entered the yard, your stillwater facade was convincing, but the moment the device registered on mine, I knew you were lessathi. No one else carries hardware built exclusively for our blood.

"The same logic applies to you," I point out. "You are lessathi as well."

Leila nods once.

"Then why are you down here?" I ask. "I was under the impression lessathi hold higher standing than lords on this continent. Couldn't your father pull strings to secure your release?"

Her gaze drops to the flagstones. When the light forms again, the letters drift lower between us:

My father and I are outcasts. We share none of their privilege. We lived in hiding for years, but months ago he was seized and sold into slavery by a local noble. I landed in these cells yesterday morning, shortly after breaking him out.

"I see."

After his capture, I feared I would never see him again, she writes. But a week ago, when the tournament began and the device started accumulating charge, I realized I had a chance. I waited until I could raise my speed, strength, and combat technique to their maximum rank of three, then attacked the estate and overwhelmed the guards holding him alone. I only wish I had waited for enough energy to invest in toughness...

She turns her forearms and shins toward the light, revealing deep purple contusions from the melee.

Before we parted, I made him promise that if the retreat failed, he would head for Ollendor without me and wait there. Ollendor is the sole city in Varathia where the lessathi hold no authority. We would be safe beyond their reach. But the city watch cut off my escape before I cleared the outer gates. I held the line until he broke through, but fatigue caught up with me. They bound me and transferred me here yesterday afternoon, shortly before your arrival.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Nineteen, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Nineteen)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Nineteen
TEXT:

#if(v_available_points == 19) {
"You mentioned waiting until you could max strength, speed, and combat technique to rank three," I say. "That means you only had nine points to allocate?"

Nine then, nine now, Leila writes. That is why I asked for privacy. When points began accumulating a week ago, I was astonished, unaware the tournament had commenced. Notices are easy to miss when you live on the fringes avoiding patrols. It was only yesterday afternoon when the king announced the opening objectives that I understood the scale of ambient magic here. The booster should be drawing substantially more power under these conditions. How many points have you accumulated so far?

"Nineteen," I answer.

Her eyes widen, and the text pauses midair:

Nineteen?

She lowers her hand, considering.

Then my hypothesis holds. The booster struggles to draw charge within this city.

"I noticed the same thing," I tell her. "I've only gained two points since entering Thilias, whereas it collected rapidly on the road, unless I was crossing completely barren wastes. Perhaps fewer tournament mages entered this city?"

Perhaps, Leila writes. Or perhaps other lessathi carry stat boosters here, dividing the available draw among us.

"You think other lessathi are using devices in Thilias?"

It is a possibility. May I inspect your device?

"Sure," I say, pulling the bronze casing from my coat and handing it over.

She turns the unit in her slender hands, inspecting the seams and dials with intense concentration. When she looks up, fresh script forms:

This is unrelated to my check, but I must ask: Barry, why haven't you allocated a single point?

}
#if(v_available_points != 19) {
"You mentioned waiting until you could max strength, speed, and combat technique to rank three," I say. "That means you only had nine points to allocate?"

Nine then, nine now, Leila writes. That is why I asked for privacy. When points began accumulating a week ago, I was astonished, unaware the tournament had commenced. Notices are easy to miss when you live on the fringes avoiding patrols. It was only yesterday afternoon when the king announced the opening objectives that I understood the scale of ambient magic here. The booster should be drawing substantially more power under these conditions. How many points have you accumulated so far?

"Nineteen," I answer.

Her eyes widen, and the text pauses midair:

Nineteen?

She lowers her hand, considering.

Then my hypothesis holds. The booster struggles to draw charge within this city.

"I noticed the same thing," I tell her. "I've only gained two points since entering Thilias, whereas it collected rapidly on the road, unless I was crossing completely barren wastes. Perhaps fewer tournament mages entered this city?"

Perhaps, Leila writes. Or perhaps other lessathi carry stat boosters here, dividing the available draw among us.

"You think other lessathi are using devices in Thilias?"

It is a possibility. May I inspect your device?

"Sure," I say, pulling the bronze casing from my coat and handing it over.

She turns the unit in her slender hands, inspecting the seams and dials with intense concentration before handing it back.

Thank you, Leila writes. I wanted to compare the draw rate between our units. Yours gathers slightly faster, though not enough to explain a ten-point difference.

}
choice(""I wanted to save them for an emergency."", B2-Ch02a-Emergency, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Emergency) if (v_available_points == 19)
choice(""If I spend them at the first scare, I never learn what the unaugmented limit actually is."", B2-Ch02a-Last, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Last) if (v_available_points == 19)
choice(""It's a Western control trial. Zero allocations. I wanted a clean unaugmented baseline."", B2-Ch02a-Average, v_ac_b2_ch2_wall = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Average) if (v_available_points == 19)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Backup, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Backup) if (v_available_points != 19)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Emergency
TEXT:

"I wanted to save them for an emergency."

In that case, I hope you consider this arena an emergency, Leila writes. Otherwise, our prospects look grim.

"You mentioned another reason for inspecting my booster?" I ask.

Yes, I wanted to compare the draw rate, Leila writes. Yours gathers slightly faster, though not enough to explain a ten-point difference.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Backup, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Backup)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Last
TEXT:

"If I spend them at the first scare, I never learn what the unaugmented limit actually is."

How you survived this far without allocated stats is a mystery, Leila writes. But I hope you reconsider before the iron gates rise. We need every advantage available if we intend to walk out.

"You mentioned another reason for inspecting my booster?" I ask.

Yes, I wanted to compare the draw rate, Leila writes. Yours gathers slightly faster, though not enough to explain a ten-point difference.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Backup, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Backup)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Average
TEXT:

achievement("Fourth wall breaker",v_ac_b2_ch2_wall)
"It's a Western control trial. Zero allocations. I wanted a clean unaugmented baseline."

A... control trial? Leila writes, tilting her head.

"Sorry," I say quickly. "I meant I am saving them for an emergency."

In that case, I hope you consider this arena an emergency, Leila writes. Otherwise, our prospects look grim.

"You mentioned another reason for inspecting my booster?" I ask.

Yes, I wanted to compare the draw rate, Leila writes. Yours gathers slightly faster, though not enough to explain a ten-point difference.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Backup, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Backup)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Backup
TEXT:

"You can tell all that just by looking at it?" I ask.

Not by sight, Leila writes. By reading the aura flow through the casing. Can't you?

"I can feel the general hum of energy," I say, "but measuring draw rates between two units is beyond me."

Curious, Leila writes. If you cannot read device flow, how did you know the booster was tailored for lessathi before entering the tournament? From what you said, you have no contact with our people. You must have reasoned it out. Unless...

She pauses, eyes narrowing slightly.

Unless you had no idea how powerful the hardware truly was, and planned to bluff your way through as a stillwater from start to finish, treating the booster as a mere backup.

choice(""I had a working theory. I did not have a lessathi textbook."", B2-Ch02a-Better, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Better)
choice(""Actually, to be honest, before coming here, I didn't even know I was a lessathi."", B2-Ch02a-Honest, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Honest)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Better
TEXT:

"I had a working theory. I did not have a lessathi textbook."

Leila's shoulders shake in a silent laugh, her palm flying up to cover her mouth.

You are a terrible liar, she writes, amusement dancing in her eyes. Your aura turns completely jagged when you twist the truth.

"My aura turns... jagged?"

I cannot fathom how reckless you are! she writes. No one in their right mind would attempt to bluff through a continental tournament! Now I understand why you look so calm after hearing what the arena master has in store. This is just an ordinary afternoon for you, isn't it?

"I wouldn't call it ordinary," I say. "Only the third time I've landed in a dungeon since arriving in Varathia. Still room for improvement."

"Hey! You two!" a guard barks through the high slit of Leila's door. "Save the private talk for lockup! This is recreation muster. Back into the yard!"

"Right away, officer," I call back, and we both step back out onto the flags.

We return to separate posts along the perimeter without another word, giving Wilhelm no further opening to listen in.

The remainder of the muster passes in heavy silence. With no knowledge of the opening trial, tactical talk is useless, and no one is in the mood for idle chatter. To kill the two hours, Bruce drops into sets of pushups, Wilhelm hones his dagger along an oilstone, I pull out my notebook to study, and the two cutthroats roll bone dice against the wall. Leila remains standing by her door, gaze fixed on the blank masonry until the bolts finally turn.

When the muster ends, we file back into our quarters. I spend the afternoon working through my creature notes on the stone slab, reviewing the behavioral patterns and weak points of various beasts recorded in my travels.

I still remember the day I commissioned the enchantment on this notebook. It was the morning I set out from the Western Continent, determined to find a path to becoming a mage. The spellwork cost a small fortune, but it was far cheaper than an expanded bottomless pack and suited my needs perfectly. A notebook with endless blank pages—the ideal weapon for a long search for knowledge.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Deal, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Honest
TEXT:

"Actually, to be honest, before coming here, I didn't even know I was a lessathi."

You mean you thought you were human? Leila writes.

"That's what I believed, yes."

Leila's shoulders shake in a silent laugh, her palm flying up to cover her mouth.

I cannot fathom how reckless you are! she writes, amusement dancing in her eyes. No one in their right mind would attempt to bluff through a continental tournament! Now I understand why you look so calm after hearing what the arena master has in store. This is just an ordinary afternoon for you, isn't it?

"I wouldn't call it ordinary," I say. "Only the third time I've landed in a dungeon since arriving in Varathia. Still room for improvement."

"Hey! You two!" a guard barks through the high slit of Leila's door. "Save the private talk for lockup! This is recreation muster. Back into the yard!"

"Right away, officer," I call back, and we both step back out onto the flags.

We return to separate posts along the perimeter without another word, giving Wilhelm no further opening to listen in.

The remainder of the muster passes in heavy silence. With no knowledge of the opening trial, tactical talk is useless, and no one is in the mood for idle chatter. To kill the two hours, Bruce drops into sets of pushups, Wilhelm hones his dagger along an oilstone, I pull out my notebook to study, and the two cutthroats roll bone dice against the wall. Leila remains standing by her door, gaze fixed on the blank masonry until the bolts finally turn.

When the muster ends, we file back into our quarters. I spend the afternoon working through my creature notes on the stone slab, reviewing the behavioral patterns and weak points of various beasts recorded in my travels.

I still remember the day I commissioned the enchantment on this notebook. It was the morning I set out from the Western Continent, determined to find a path to becoming a mage. The spellwork cost a small fortune, but it was far cheaper than an expanded bottomless pack and suited my needs perfectly. A notebook with endless blank pages—the ideal weapon for a long search for knowledge.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Deal, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Deal
TEXT:

I flip through the parchment, categorizing potential hazards the arena master might unleash. Harpies, wargs, wyverns, cerberus hounds, mammoths, giant arachnids, roc eagles—the catalog runs endlessly. Subspecies matter as well; a red cave spider behaves very differently from a white-spotted forest stalker or a ten-legged web-spinner, each demanding distinct counters and footwork.

Then there is the matter of humanoid monsters. Common folk dismiss them as mindless brutes, but the reality is far more dangerous. Goblin and ogre tribes organize disciplined raiding parties, using complex dialects and coordinated tactics. If the under-tunnels hold champions on par with the ogre fort commander or the shaman, the sands will be a slaughterhouse.

A low voice from the ventilation slit cuts through my thoughts.

"Greetings."

I turn toward the narrow window, but the speaker remains out of sight against the outer corridor wall.

"Do I know you?" I ask.

"No," the voice replies smoothly. "Word reached our circle that a fellow lessathi was remanded to the cells last night. I came to pay my respects."

"A fellow lessathi."

"Indeed. I bring an arrangement. We rarely traffic with common prisoners, but given your heritage, an exception is warranted."

He pauses, allowing the words to settle.

"Go on," I say.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Soundproof, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Soundproof)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Soundproof
TEXT:

"We could not help but observe," the voice continues, "that the King of Thilias has extended remarkable trust to your company. Enough to grant you entry into the private chamber beneath his throne. Such access is not easily obtained."

He pauses, waiting for a reply. When I offer none, he resumes.

"The arrangement is straightforward. Persuade the king to admit you to his vault once more, carrying a small device of our manufacture."

"Is this device explosive by any chance?" I ask.

A soft, refined chuckle drifts through the masonry.

"Hardly. If our objective were regicide, far simpler and cleaner methods exist."

#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0) {
"Then what is your objective?"

"We wish to subtly adjust the function of one of the gnome's inventions. An acoustic dampener currently renders that chamber entirely soundproof, denying us insight into royal councils. At present, we possess no operative whom the king trusts sufficiently to grant access. Thus, we turn to you. We do not ask for charity; we can ensure your matches in the arena are considerably more manageable. Agree to assist us, and we handle the rest."

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 1) {
"Then what is your objective?"

"We wish to subtly adjust the function of one of the gnome's inventions. An acoustic dampener currently renders that chamber entirely soundproof, denying us insight into royal councils. At present, we possess no operative whom the king trusts sufficiently to grant access. Thus, we turn to you. We do not ask for charity; we can ensure your matches in the arena are considerably more manageable. Agree to assist us, and we handle the rest."

I already pledged to help Golmyck rid his city of the lessathi cabal. But this operative says all I need to do to reap the arena advantages is agree. Since I plan to strike them down later anyway, a false agreement costs nothing.

}
choice("Lie to the lessathi about accepting the deal and betray them later.", B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_b2_ch2_deal = 1)
choice("Refuse the lessathi's offer", B2-Ch02a-Refuse-threat, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Refuse-threat) if (v_b2_ch2_tell_servant == 1)
choice("Refuse the lessathi's offer", B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_b2_ch2_deal = 2) if (v_b2_ch2_tell_servant == 0)
choice("Accept the lessathi's offer", B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_ac_b2_ch2_easy = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_b2_ch2_deal = 3) if (v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Refuse-threat
TEXT:

"No, thanks," I say. "I prefer a challenge."

A heavy pause hangs in the narrow space between the bars.

"Is that your final word?" the lessathi asks.

"It is."

"While I mentioned we could ease your passage through the trials," he says, tone dropping an octave, "I omitted the corollary: we can ensure your matches are substantially more lethal should you refuse."

"How so?"

"We could direct the guards to seize your stat booster, stripping you of all defense."

"I don't know how you learned of the device," I say, "but possession violates no arena regulation."

"I never claimed it was illegal," the voice replies smoothly. "Only that we would have it stripped away. A quiet conversation with the arena master bends any rule for a special occasion. Do we have an arrangement, or not?"

choice("Lie to the lessathi about accepting the deal and betray them later.", B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_b2_ch2_deal = 1)
choice("Refuse the lessathi's offer", B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_b2_ch2_deal = 4)
choice("Accept the lessathi's offer", B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion, v_b2_ch2_deal = 3) if (v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Deal-conclusion
TEXT:

achievement("The easy way out",v_ac_b2_ch2_easy)
#if(v_b2_ch2_deal == 1) {
"Well, sir, you have yourself a deal," I say.

"Excellent," the lessathi replies. "Here is the component."

A small, stamped metal plate slips through the high ventilation slit, clattering onto the flagstones. It resembles copper alloy, etched with fine lessathi script along the bevel.

"Guard it carefully," the voice adds. "Procuring a duplicate would prove difficult."

Footsteps shift in the corridor.

"One final matter: expect a late addition to your division roster before the matches commence. He will resolve the bulk of your difficulties."

The footsteps recede down the gallery. I wait until the outer passage falls dead silent, then return to my stone slab and pick up the notebook.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_deal == 2) {
"No, thanks," I say. "I prefer a challenge."

A brief silence lingers on the other side of the masonry.

"Is that your final word?" the lessathi asks.

"It is."

The voice maintains its smooth, unruffled cadence.

"Very well. In that case, I anticipate your performance on the sand. I doubt our paths will cross again. Farewell, fellow lessathi."

His footsteps fade down the corridor. I wait until the gallery is quiet before returning to my notes on the stone slab.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_deal == 3) {
"I suppose we have an agreement," I say.

"Excellent," the lessathi replies. "Here is the component."

A small, stamped metal plate slips through the high ventilation slit, clattering onto the flagstones. It resembles copper alloy, etched with fine lessathi script along the bevel.

"Guard it carefully," the voice adds. "Procuring a duplicate would prove difficult."

Footsteps shift in the corridor.

"One final matter: expect a late addition to your division roster before the matches commence. He will resolve the bulk of your difficulties."

The footsteps recede down the gallery. I wait until the outer passage falls dead silent, then return to my stone slab and pick up the notebook.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_deal == 4) {
"No, sorry," I say. "Better luck next time."

A brief silence lingers on the other side of the masonry.

"Very well," the lessathi says calmly. "In that case, I anticipate your performance on the sand. I doubt our paths will cross again. Farewell, fellow lessathi."

His footsteps fade down the corridor. I wait until the gallery is quiet before returning to my notes on the stone slab.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Transceivers, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Transceivers)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Transceivers
TEXT:

set(v_b2_ch2_heard,1) if (v_hearing >= 3)
set(v_b2_ch2_heard,0) if (v_hearing < 3)
#if(v_hearing >= 3) {
Not long after the operative departs, low voices drift from the far end of the outer corridor. I recognize the rough rasp of the guard from morning muster.

"Hear about the council this morning?" the guard mutters.

"The one between the master and the ambassador?" a second voice asks.

"Aye," the guard says. "They are rolling out the minotaur for round two."

"The minotaur?" the second gasps. "The undefeated arena champion?"

"The one and only," the guard chuckles. "And giant scorpions and manticores for the opening round."

"What about round three? Still gladiator duels?"

"Survivors slaughter each other one-on-one at the end," the guard says. "Though no one expects a single dog to make it past the scorpions."

Their voices fade into harsh laughter as the patrol moves out of range.

Giant scorpions and manticores. I have detailed behavioral diagrams and venom counters recorded in my notes. At least we will not enter the ring blind.

I spend the rest of the afternoon reviewing the anatomy of both species. When my eyes tire, I dial Daren, Kate, and Hadrik in sequence. Daren vents about his squad mates, dismissing them as spineless mercenaries hunting quick coin. Kate mentions nearly decapitating an aggressive cellmate during muster, halting only when informed of the division penalty; no one has dared approach her since.

Hadrik is having a far better time. He has already turned most of his squad into drinking companions, keeping them in the yard long past the mandatory two hours.

I brief all three on the guard gossip, passing along the specific weak points and strike angles for scorpions and manticores from my notes.

}
#if(v_hearing < 3) {
I spend the rest of the afternoon working through creature lore. When my eyes tire, I dial Daren, Kate, and Hadrik in sequence on the transceiver. Daren vents about his squad mates, dismissing them as spineless mercenaries hunting quick coin. Kate mentions nearly decapitating an aggressive cellmate during muster, halting only when informed of the division penalty; no one has dared approach her since.

Hadrik is having a far better time. He has already turned most of his squad into drinking companions, keeping them in the yard long past the mandatory two hours.

I ask each of them if they have gathered any intelligence on the trial format, but their wings know no more than mine. The organizers are keeping the match parameters sealed until the gates lift.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Tactical, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Tactical)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Tactical
TEXT:

After signing off, I return to my studies until the heavy creak of the intake gate echoes through the yard.

"Farewell," the clerk's flat drone drifts through the masonry, followed by the solid slam of the outer door.

I ease my cell door open to inspect the newcomer. The man crossing the common room radiates intense magical energy, making no effort to dampen the aura. A glowing non-combatant sigil floats above his crown, marking him as a local mage remanded for breaking Varathian law rather than a tournament entrant. He catches me looking, offers not so much as a nod, and marches directly into his assigned quarters.

Night settles quietly over the wing, punctuated only by a slurred call from Hadrik on the transceiver, who insists on handing the horn to an intoxicated squad mate doing a surprisingly accurate impression of a Western Continent high lord.

At dawn, the familiar guard rattles the iron grille.

"Up!" he barks. "Recreation muster! Into the yard!"

I lock my door and make for my usual corner of the perimeter. A few yards away, the two cutthroats are cornering Leila where she leans against the masonry, observing them in silence.

"Hey, princess," one sneers. "Why not invite both of us into your room like you did with the stillwater yesterday?"

"Aye," the second adds, grinning. "We have vital tactical matters to discuss."

Leila watches them with flat disinterest. The first thug scowls at the lack of reaction and slams both palms against the stones on either side of her head, leaning in close.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Knocks, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Knocks)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Knocks
TEXT:

"Listen, princess," the thug sneers. "Drop the mute routine. I know you hear every word. Wise up and do what I say, or I will hurt you so badly you'll wish—"

He never finishes the sentence. Leila's right fist drives straight into his solar plexus with explosive speed. The blow drops him instantly, gasping and curling into a ball on the flags.

"You bitch!" the second cutthroat roars, reaching for his hip, but a lightning-fast spinning heel kick from Leila catches him under the jaw. The impact launches him backward into the stone wall, and he slumps to the floor unconscious.

"By the gods, look at that!" Bruce roars from across the yard.

He charges across the flags and clamps both heavy hands onto Leila's shoulders. She starts, tensing slightly, but reads the genuine admiration on his face and keeps her daggers sheathed.

"With hands like that, you could dismantle a squad on your own!" Bruce barks, grinning from ear to ear. "How did the city watch ever drag you in?"

He catches himself, realizing he is crowding her, and pulls his hands back with an awkward clearing of his throat.

"Apologies," Bruce mutters. "I know you keep your own counsel. But know this: I have your back. When the match turns ugly, look for me. That is all."

Leila blinks, offering a small, measured nod.

"Good," Bruce grunts. "I'll be by the pillars if you need steel."

He stomps back to his post. The first thug drags himself upright, wheezing, and hauls his limp partner back to their corner, neither daring to look in Leila's direction.

The remainder of the muster passes in silence. The remanded mage keeps to his wall, and Wilhelm leaves him entirely alone.

Once we return to lockup, I settle onto my stone slab and reopen the notebook. A few minutes into my reading, three light raps tap against the oak door.

"Who is it?" I ask.

Silence. After a few seconds, three heavier knocks echo against the iron bracing.

choice("Open the door.", B2-Ch02a-Open, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Open, v_b2_ch2_upset = 0)
choice("Tell them that I'm not opening the door until I get an answer.", B2-Ch02a-Answer, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Answer, v_b2_ch2_upset = 1)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Open
TEXT:

"All right, hold on," I say, closing the notebook and swinging my legs off the slab.

I turn the iron key and pull the door inward. Leila stands on the threshold, her expression unreadable.

"Oh," I say. "It's you."

Glowing blue letters shimmer into being between us:

Come with me. There is something I need to show you.

"Sure. Let me lock up."

I secure my door and follow her across the common room. Once inside her quarters, she slides the bolt home and points toward the masonry behind her stone slab.

Blue script illuminates the dark:

Place your palm on that wall. Tell me what you sense.

I press my hand flat against the dressed stone, but feel nothing beyond cold rock.

"Can you give me a hint? What am I looking for?"

Don't look with your eyes, she writes. Feel it. Focus your aura through your palm as you touch the stone, and you will understand.

I channel my focus into my hand, and a faint, harmonic pulse hums through the stone, the masonry vibrating in direct resonance with my aura.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Passage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Passage)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Answer
TEXT:

"If you won't answer, the door stays shut," I call out.

No reply. Three more knocks rattle the timber. I ignore them, turning a page in my notebook.

The visitor changes tactics, tapping steadily against the wood once every second, regular as a clock. After a full minute of relentless rhythm, I toss the notebook onto the slab, cross the cell, and yank the door open.

"What do you—"

The words die in my throat. Leila stands in the doorway, staring up at me with an unmistakable scowl.

"Oh..." I mutter. "Right."

Without a word, her small hand clamps onto my forearm with surprising strength, hauling me out into the corridor.

"Wait! Let me lock the room!"

She halts, grip unyielding, waiting while I work the key into the lock. The moment the bolt turns, she tugs me along again, keeping her eyes fixed forward.

"Look, I'm sorry," I say. "I forgot you couldn't call out."

She gives no sign of hearing, pulling me straight to her door. She releases my wrist only to draw her own key, unlocks the cell, swings the door wide, and jerks her chin toward the interior with an impatient glare.

"Going, going," I say, stepping inside.

She secures the bolt, marches over, grabs my wrist, and slaps my palm flat against the dressed stone behind her bed, fixing me with an expectant look.

"Is there... something special about this wall?" I ask.

Yes, glowing blue letters form between us.

"Can you give me a hint? What am I looking for?"

Don't look with your eyes, she writes. Feel it. Focus your aura through your palm as you touch the stone, and you will understand.

I channel my focus into my hand, and a faint, harmonic pulse hums through the stone, the masonry vibrating in direct resonance with my aura.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Passage, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Passage)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Passage
TEXT:

"You're right," I tell her. "There is an active matrix in the rock. But it sits idle. Like half a mechanism waiting for a trigger."

Leila nods once.

The opposite wall carries an identical rune pattern, she writes. These stones react specifically to lessathi auras, acting as twin switches requiring simultaneous contact from two lessathi.

"Switches for what?"

To open a descent into the cavern network below these tunnels, she writes. Lessathi architecture frequently employs this mechanism for hidden vaults. Word in the yard says the arena master keeps his menagerie chained in the lower caverns. If we slip down, we can scout the threats ahead of time. Or better yet: sabotage the entire spectacle by executing the beasts in their pens before the gates lift.

"Tempting," I admit. "What happens if we are caught? Disqualification for cheating?"

No arena rule forbids culling beasts before a match, Leila writes. However...

The blue letters pause midair.

"However what?"

Arena regulations explicitly state that participant safety is not guaranteed outside designated division quarters.

"Meaning if the handlers catch us, they can loose every beast in the pens with no restrictions."

Leila nods.

We can scout the entrance, she writes. If patrols are heavy, we return to our cells.

I weigh the risks for a moment.

"Fair enough. One look, then we decide."

She nods, crosses to the opposite wall near the door, and raises her palm toward the masonry.

Are you ready? she writes.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Swim, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Swim)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Swim
TEXT:

"As ready as I'll ever be, I suppose," I say.

Leila presses her palm against the stone. A sharp current of raw magic rips through my palm, and the floor drops away beneath our boots into empty air.

We plummet through darkness for what feels like a hundred feet while the ceiling trapdoor slides shut with a distant boom. The fall ends in an icy, bone-jarring impact as we slam into the black water of a subterranean lake.

I kick toward the surface, gasping as I break the water. Luminous blue crystals studded across the soaring vault cast a pale cobalt glow across the cavern. High above, four heavy hemp cables dangle from ceiling pulleys, but there are no handholds or ledges along the sheer walls to climb back to the cells.

A frantic thrashing churns the water behind me. I spin around to find Leila flailing desperately, her head dipping beneath the surface, mouth open in a soundless scream. She cannot swim.

I cut through the water with hard strokes, catching her under the shoulders to stop the wild thrashing.

"Leila, hold onto me!" I shout over the splashing. "Wrap your arms around my neck. I've got us."

Panic widens her eyes, but she locks both arms around my neck, clinging tightly. I stroke steadily toward the rocky shoreline and haul us both onto the gravel shingle.

We collapse on the stones, coughing and shivering in the clammy cavern air. I turn to survey our surroundings and freeze: a few hundred paces along the shore sits a squared wooden cabin, with a neatly stacked cord of dry split logs beside the door.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Dragging, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Dragging)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Dragging
TEXT:

A small hand tugs my sodden sleeve. Leila is kneeling in the gravel, wet silver hair plastered across her cheeks and her head bowed low.

Glowing blue letters shimmer in the dim light:

I'm sorry.

"About what?" I ask.

About dragging you down here.

"Leila," I say, "if you hadn't brought me along, you would have drowned in that lake."

She stares down at the wet stones as the letters dissolve.

"Listen to me," I say. "There is nothing to apologize for. Neither of us expected a drop-trap. If anything, we are right where we wanted to be—in the menagerie vaults. The only change is that now we need to locate an exit while we hunt the pens."

My gaze drifts back to the four heavy cables descending into the black water. The square spacing suggests a submerged platform at the bottom of the pool. An elevator, perhaps.

choice("Swim to the bottom of the lake to see if I can find a way to start the elevator.", B2-Ch02a-Cabin, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Cabin, v_b2_ch2_elevator = 1)
choice("Ignore the elevator and go directly for the wooden cabin to start a fire.", B2-Ch02a-Cabin, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Cabin, v_b2_ch2_elevator = 0)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Cabin
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_elevator == 1) {
"See those cables?" I ask, pointing out across the water. "They look anchored to a platform on the lakebed. I'm going down to check for a release lever or gear switch. Wait here."

Be careful, she writes.

I dive back into the icy water and stroke toward the center of the pool. Drawing a deep breath, I submerge and follow the cables down into the blue-lit depths. A heavy timber-and-iron platform rests flat against the bottom, but there are no cranks or levers along the frame. Beside the platform stands a solitary granite pillar, its crown hollowed into an intricate, spider-shaped keyhole.

Without a spider key, the lift is useless. With my lungs burning for air, I kick hard for the surface and haul myself back onto the shingle.

Did you find anything? Leila writes as I wring out my shirt.

"No levers," I pant. "Just a granite pillar with a spider-shaped lock. Useless without the key. Let's make for the cabin and search for flint."

Leila nods, and we make our way across the gravel. The existence of the cabin is strange. Timber does not grow in underground caverns; whoever engineered the drop-trap must maintain this staging post from another approach. Stacked firewood right beside the landing pool feels deliberate, almost hospitable.

The cabin interior is bare of hearth or chimney, but a bundle of dry kindling and a striker with flint sit on a timber shelf. I carry the wood and striker outside, scrape dried lichen from the cave walls for tinder, and spark a blaze. The fire takes quickly, and we sit close to the crackling flames, letting the heat drive off the damp chill.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_elevator == 0) {
"Let's make for the cabin first," I say. "We need dry wood and flint before hypothermia sets in."

Leila nods, and we make our way across the gravel. The existence of the cabin is strange. Timber does not grow in underground caverns; whoever engineered the drop-trap must maintain this staging post from another approach. Stacked firewood right beside the landing pool feels deliberate, almost hospitable.

The cabin interior is bare of hearth or chimney, but a bundle of dry kindling and a striker with flint sit on a timber shelf. I carry the wood and striker outside, scrape dried lichen from the cave walls for tinder, and spark a blaze. The fire takes quickly, and we sit close to the crackling flames, letting the heat drive off the damp chill.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Natural, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Natural)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Natural
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_upset == 0) {
"Not bad for an improvised hearth," I tell Leila as the heat soaks into our sodden clothes. "A little flint and dry cave lichen go a long way."

Leila nods, turning her back to the flames to dry her long silver hair without scorching the tips.

"I've been wondering," I say, watching the firelight catch the pale strands. "Is silver your natural hair color? I've never seen hair like that before, though my experience with lessathi is limited."

She goes completely still, staring out into the dark cavern. Wet bangs fall across her brow, shadowing her expression.

"If it's private, forget I asked," I add.

No, it is fine... she writes slowly.

The blue letters fade, pausing before the next line forms:

Have you ever heard of the Beacon of Hope?

"The research facility experimenting on artificial mages? Yes," I say. "Did you work there?"

She shakes her head.

No, the script reads. I was one of the failed subjects. My hair lost its pigment gradually, bleached by years of arcane experiments trying to force a magical awakening. It used to be pitch black.

"Gods," I murmur. "Is that why you lost your voice?"

No, Leila writes. I was born mute. And I suffered for it. I remember little from early childhood, but I remember what the other lessathi said: that our race is pure and free of birth defects, and that my muteness proved I was a half-blood. Even the man who sired me had doubts. He never treated me as a true daughter, blaming my mother for dying and leaving him with a defective child. That was why he volunteered me to the Beacon researchers without a second thought.

"You say the man who sired you," I note. "Earlier you said you landed in these cells after breaking your father out of slavery. The man you rescued... is he someone else?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_upset == 1) {
"Not bad for an improvised hearth," I tell Leila as the heat soaks into our sodden clothes. "A little flint and dry cave lichen go a long way."

Leila nods.

"Are you still upset about earlier?" I ask. "When I kept the door locked?"

She blinks, looking puzzled.

No, she writes. I had already forgotten it.

She turns her back to the flames to dry her long silver hair without scorching the tips.

"I've been wondering," I say, watching the firelight catch the pale strands. "Is silver your natural hair color? I've never seen hair like that before, though my experience with lessathi is limited."

She goes completely still, staring out into the dark cavern. Wet bangs fall across her brow, shadowing her expression.

"If it's private, forget I asked," I add.

No, it is fine... she writes slowly.

The blue letters fade, pausing before the next line forms:

Have you ever heard of the Beacon of Hope?

"The research facility experimenting on artificial mages? Yes," I say. "Did you work there?"

She shakes her head.

No, the script reads. I was one of the failed subjects. My hair lost its pigment gradually, bleached by years of arcane experiments trying to force a magical awakening. It used to be pitch black.

"Gods," I murmur. "Is that why you lost your voice?"

No, Leila writes. I was born mute. And I suffered for it. I remember little from early childhood, but I remember what the other lessathi said: that our race is pure and free of birth defects, and that my muteness proved I was a half-blood. Even the man who sired me had doubts. He never treated me as a true daughter, blaming my mother for dying and leaving him with a defective child. That was why he volunteered me to the Beacon researchers without a second thought.

"You say the man who sired you," I note. "Earlier you said you landed in these cells after breaking your father out of slavery. The man you rescued... is he someone else?"

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Hiding, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Hiding)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Hiding
TEXT:

The man I rescued is the one I have called father for the past ten years, Leila writes. He pulled me from the Beacon when I had surrendered all hope.

"Is that why you both became outcasts?" I ask.

Leila nods once.

He was a caretaker at the facility, she writes. When he could no longer stomach the cruelty, he resolved to help us escape, knowing full well it would mark him as an exile for the rest of his life.

"Did anyone else escape with you?"

Leila shakes her head.

He tried to convince other children, but fear ran too deep. They thought it was a warden's trick. One of the orphans betrayed his plan to the overseers, forcing him to flee immediately. He unlocked every cell block as he ran, but I was the only one who followed him through the gates.

"Did they hunt you?"

Relentlessly, she writes. We lived like fugitives in the Varathian wilderness for years, moving camp every few days to throw hounds off our trail. After months of scavenging, my father assembled a suggestion ward. Suggestion spells implant impulses into foreign minds, making the target believe the thought is their own. His device broadcast a field that made anyone approaching our valley feel an overwhelming urge to turn around.

"I know that spell well," I say. "A companion of mine casts it constantly."

The ward let us build a permanent home, Leila writes. A wooden cabin by a river, with a modest garden. Some wildlife proved immune, but they grew accustomed to our presence. A few even brought us wild fruit on occasion.

"How was he captured, then? Did the ward fail?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Meridith, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Meridith)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Meridith
TEXT:

No, Leila writes. We left the forest eventually. Word reached us that lessathi leadership had shifted—their new leader, Meridith, prohibited outcast hunts. My father wanted to move to a city to acquire specialized parts for his workshop. I didn't understand the obsession with hardware until my sixteenth birthday, when he gifted me this stat booster. Back then it transcribed only halting phrases, but he refined the runes year after year until it captured my full thoughts.

"Wait," I gasp. "The booster!"

What? she writes.

"The device!" I yank the bronze casing from my coat pocket. "I completely forgot about the hardware!"

I shake the unit vigorously to clear any trapped moisture, then rapidly toggle through the selectors without locking points to verify the relays still respond.

Leila watches my frantic checks and breaks into a silent giggle, shoulders shaking.

It takes far more than a dunk in a lake to disable lessathi hardware, she writes. Rest easy. Your booster is undamaged.

"Sorry," I say, tucking the device back into my coat. "I shouldn't have interrupted. How was your father taken?"

The decree banned hunting outcasts, but it did not erase old grudges, Leila writes. Former Beacon overseers had waited years to settle the score. Unable to kill him openly, they forged debt registers claiming years of unpaid rent on our townhouse, bribing the landlord to testify. We had no standing in court, while they possessed forged seals and false witnesses. Bound by legal debt, my father was auctioned as a slave to a Thilias high noble. I slipped through the cordon before they could seize me, living in the alleys for months trying to find a way into the manor. The tournament's energy gave me the power to strike.

I look at her in the firelight. I am traveling with Kate, another survivor of the Beacon. Should I tell her? Kate never mentioned Leila, only Diane. Did Kate believe Leila was dead, or did she despise her as a lessathi? I hesitate to raise false hopes if Kate wants nothing to do with her past.

choice("Tell her about Kate", B2-Ch02a-Tunnels, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Tunnels, v_b2_ch2_kate = 1)
choice("Don't tell her about Kate", B2-Ch02a-Tunnels, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Tunnels, v_b2_ch2_kate = 0)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Tunnels
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 1) {
"I know about the Beacon because of someone I travel with," I say. "Do you remember a girl named Kate from the orphanage?"

Leila nods immediately.

I remember her, she writes. She was always with the elementalist siblings who later joined the lessathi elite guard. Word was she perished in the jungle along with the rest of the surviving orphans when the facility closed...

"The rumor was false," I tell her. "Kate survived. She is traveling with me in the tournament right now. Would you like to see her?"

Joy lights up Leila's face, but the brightness dims almost immediately, her gaze turning toward the dark.

I doubt she would welcome me, Leila writes. I was kept in an isolated cell block, so we spoke rarely. Besides, after everything lessathi did to her at the Beacon, she likely hates my kind.

"You won't know until you ask," I tell her. "I'm lessathi too, and she hasn't frozen me yet."

She offers no answer, keeping her eyes on the stones.

"It's settled," I say, standing up from the gravel. "The moment we clear these vaults, I will call her on the transceiver and set up a reunion. Let's move. Our clothes are dry enough."

Leila nods hesitantly, takes my outstretched hand, and lets me pull her to her feet. We leave the dying embers behind and step into the tunnels beyond the cabin. For the next twenty minutes we advance in silence through the cavern network, using a stout timber pole scavenged from the woodpile to probe the ground for pressure plates. Where the passages narrow into pitch darkness, our glowing stat booster screens and the scattered cobalt crystals in the masonry provide enough light to keep our footing.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 0) {
"Your father is waiting in Ollendor, right?" I ask.

Leila nods.

"Then what are we waiting for?" I say, rising from the gravel. "Let's find an exit and get you on the road to Ollendor. Come on, our clothes are dry enough."

Leila nods, takes my outstretched hand, and lets me pull her to her feet. We leave the dying embers behind and step into the tunnels beyond the cabin. For the next twenty minutes we advance in silence through the cavern network, using a stout timber pole scavenged from the woodpile to probe the ground for pressure plates. Where the passages narrow into pitch darkness, our glowing stat booster screens and the scattered cobalt crystals in the masonry provide enough light to keep our footing.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Creatures, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Creatures)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Creatures
TEXT:

The passage widens into a broad subterranean gallery flanked on both sides by iron-barred holding pens. Behind the rusted grating, caged horrors pace and snarl in the cobalt light: dire wolves, horned gargoyles, giant tunnel rats, screeching harpies, chained griffins, and sabre-toothed cats. A fraction of the menagerie the arena master has assembled beneath the sands.

No guards, Leila writes. They expected no approach from this direction. A chance to slip past unnoticed.

She bends, scoops a jagged stone from the gravel, and tosses it lightly in her palm while eyeing the bars. The rock fits cleanly between the iron uprights. With her augmented strength, a clean throw would cave in a beast's skull.

The question, the blue script glimmers: do we cull these monsters while they are caged, or focus solely on finding an exit?

choice("Kill the creatures.", B2-Ch02a-Kill, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Kill, v_b2_ch2_creatures = 1)
choice("Leave the creatures alone, and concentrate on finding a way out.", B2-Ch02a-Leave, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Leave, v_b2_ch2_creatures = 0)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Kill
TEXT:

"We cull them," I say. "That was the objective. But quietly—we can't afford to alert the handlers."

Quietly, then, Leila writes, and snaps her wrist.

The stone rockets through the bars, striking a pacing sabre-toothed cat square between the eyes. Bone shatters with a wet crunch, and the predator collapses in a heap. She draws back a second rock, but across the gallery, the harpies erupt into deafening, ear-splitting shrieks. Scaled, winged horrors with the twisted torsos of women, their screeching alerts the entire wing.

Leila redirects her aim, driving stones into the harpy cage, but the noise triggers a cascading roar across every pen in the corridor. Bellows, snarls, and the rattle of iron shake the masonry. If the handlers have a master release mechanism, we will be surrounded in seconds.

The wolves, rats, and big cats are deadly, but mundane. Leila is already suppressing the harpies, leaving me to choose between the griffins and the gargoyles.

Griffins rely on aerial dives, severely restricted by the low ceiling vaults. Gargoyles are winged, horned brutes built like sinewy ogres, boasting bone-crushing physical power in close quarters.

I target the gargoyles. Scooping a heavy stone from the path, I whip it straight through the bars at the nearest horned brow.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Gargoyles, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Gargoyles)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Gargoyles
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_combat_technique >= 3 || v_reflexes >= 3 || v_strength >= 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_combat_technique < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 && v_strength < 2)
#if(v_strength >= 2) {
The rock crushes the gargoyle's skull, dropping it instantly. I grab a second stone and drop the second monster with the same brutal force. Before I can find a third, a loud metallic groan reverberates through the tunnel as every cell gate lifts simultaneously.

The surviving gargoyle bounds through the open gate, talons clawing for my chest. I draw the enchanted dagger and sweep the blade in a clean, rising arc, severing the monster's head from its shoulders in a single stroke.

}
#if(v_combat_technique >= 3 && v_strength < 2) {
The rock clips the gargoyle's brow with a sharp crack, drawing blood but failing to drop it. The beast catches the second stone out of the air. A harsh metallic groan echoes down the corridor, and every cell gate swings open at once.

The wounded gargoyle bounds straight toward me, reaching for my throat.

I read the trajectory, pivot inside the lunging arm, and drive the enchanted dagger straight between its ribs into the heart. The other two gargoyles rush to flank me. I heave the dying carcass into the path of the nearest brute, then charge the other. A sharp feint to the chest draws its guard down before I reverse the edge, opening its throat with a lightning slash.

The final gargoyle lunges from my blind spot. Keeping my stance low, I duck beneath the sweeping claws, rise into its guard, and drive the dagger through its eye socket into the brain.

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 3 && v_combat_technique < 3 && v_strength < 2) {
The rock clips the gargoyle's brow with a sharp crack, drawing blood but failing to drop it. The beast catches the second stone out of the air. A harsh metallic groan echoes down the corridor, and every cell gate swings open at once.

The wounded gargoyle lunges straight for my throat.

It closes the distance with shocking speed, but my instincts fire faster. I slip the grasp by an inch. My creature notes noted that gargoyles prefer pack flanking unless provoked; this one charges alone only because of the stone.

I retreat two paces, drawing the enraged brute into open space. As it overcommits to another swipe, I duck inside the arc and drive my enchanted blade deep into its neck. The other two circle wide, trying to establish cross-angles. I don't give them the opening: darting forward, I slash the leathery wing of the left beast, throwing its balance onto the stones, and execute it with a thrust to the throat. The third charges, but my dagger opens its jugular before the claws can close.

}
#if(v_combat_technique < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 && v_strength < 2) {
The rock clips the gargoyle's brow with a sharp crack, drawing blood but failing to drop it. The beast catches the second stone out of the air. A harsh metallic groan echoes down the corridor, and every cell gate swings open at once.

The wounded gargoyle lunges straight for my throat.

It moves far too fast. Heavy claws clamp around my neck, lifting me off the flags. Red eyes glare into mine. I claw for my dagger, but talons crush inward, shattering my windpipe in an instant. Darkness rushes in as consciousness fades.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Griffins, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Griffins) if (v_combat_technique >= 3 || v_reflexes >= 3 || v_strength >= 2)
choice("Restart game", B2-Ch02a-Griffins, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Griffins, special:restart) if (v_combat_technique < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 && v_strength < 2)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_combat_technique < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 && v_strength < 2)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_combat_technique < 3 && v_reflexes < 3 && v_strength < 2)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Griffins
TEXT:

With the gargoyles down, my focus shifts to the four griffins. Down the corridor, Leila is locked in a tight melee against the dire wolves and sabre-toothed cats, using fluid footwork and short blades to keep them at bay; she cannot handle four winged predators on her flank.

Taking all four head-on in open space is suicide. I need a distraction. Remembering my creature lore, griffins possess the predatory instincts of raptors and have almost certainly been starved by their handlers to heighten aggression on the sands. If I can draw the giant rats toward my position, the griffins will lock onto easy prey.

I drop flat against the flagstones with my back to the rat pens, playing dead. Several rodents creep forward, sniffing the air, followed quickly by the rest of the pack scrambling over each other to reach me.

The griffins haven't reacted yet. If I draw blood from a rat, the scent might trigger their hunting reflex.

choice("Slice one of the rats with my dagger, when it comes close.", B2-Ch02a-Slice, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Slice)
choice("When the rats approach, get up slowly, so I don't scare them away.", B2-Ch02a-Slowly, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Slowly)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Slice
TEXT:

As the lead rat scurries into reach, I whip my dagger down, pinning it to the stones through the ribs. It lets out a piercing squeal, thrashing in a pool of dark blood. The rest of the pack scatters in terror.

The shriek and the scent of fresh blood draw all four griffins at once. Feathery crests flare, and they advance in a coordinated wedge directly toward me. The live prey bait backfired completely.

I snatch the dead rat by its tail and hurl it down the corridor. The griffins don't even twitch their heads, keeping their predatory gaze locked on me. Raptors prefer live, moving targets over carrion.

I glance back at the open gargoyle cell. The iron frame is narrow, admitting only one beast at a time. If I hold the doorway, I can bottleneck their numbers and take them on one by one.

choice("Lure the griffins inside the gargoyle cell, and hold them off at the door.", B2-Ch02a-Lure, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Lure)
choice("Fight them in the tunnel, where there's more space to move around.", B2-Ch02a-Space, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Space)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Lure
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_toughness >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_toughness < 3)
#if(v_toughness >= 3) {
I back into the gargoyle cell, planting my boots in the narrow doorway with my enchanted blade raised. The lead griffin lunges through the frame, wings scraping iron, blocking the entrance for its pack. I thrust for its chest, but a heavy front talon parries the blade, jamming the edge between its claws. The beast drives its razor beak straight down into my sternum.

The impact lands like an iron pickaxe, tearing the linen of my shirt and piercing skin, but dense muscle halts the penetration. Pain flares, sharp and hot, but my ribs hold firm. Gritting my teeth, I wrench the dagger free from its claws, pivot inside its guard, and bury the steel into its left eye socket. The griffin screeches and staggers; I reverse the stroke, severing its throat in one clean sweep.

The carcass slumps into the doorway, creating a barricade. The smell of blood from my shallow chest wound drives the remaining beasts into a frenzy. A second griffin scrambles clumsily over the dead body, off balance; I step inside its reach and drive my dagger straight through its heart.

The remaining two griffins hesitate, rattled by the rapid slaughter. One turns away, opting to chase scattering tunnel rats down the hall. I leap over the dead bodies, charging the third beast before it can retreat, driving my blade into its chest. Pivoting instantly, I sprint down the gallery after the final griffin, slash both its rear hocks to take out its legs, and finish it with a clean thrust to the base of the skull.

}
#if(v_toughness < 3) {
I back into the gargoyle cell, planting my boots in the narrow doorway with my enchanted blade raised. The lead griffin lunges through the frame, wings scraping iron, blocking the entrance for its pack. I thrust for its chest, but a heavy front talon parries the blade, jamming the edge between its claws. The beast drives its razor beak straight down into my sternum.

The beak punches through flesh and bone like parchment. Agony explodes across my chest as the predator tears inward. I lurch backward, losing my grip on the dagger, but a sweeping talon catches my throat, ending everything.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic) if (v_toughness >= 3)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_toughness < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_toughness < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_toughness < 3)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Space
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_agility >= 3 || v_strength >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_agility < 3 && v_strength < 3)
#if(v_agility >= 3) {
Refusing to be trapped in a cell, I hold my ground in the gallery.

Speed is my edge. I dart between flanking vectors, cutting angles and staying in continuous motion. One griffin surges ahead of the pack to pin me, but overextending isolates it from the others. I sidestep its dive and drive my blade repeatedly into its exposed flank until the beast collapses, bleeding heavily onto the flags.

Two more close in tandem. I drag my blade through the dying beast's blood, sprint forward, and flick a crimson arc straight into the eyes of the left griffin. Blinded and screeching, it thrashes wildly. I use that split second to slip beneath the right beast's beak, driving my dagger through its throat. Spinning back, I plant the blade into the blinded griffin's skull as it wipes its eyes. The final beast freezes in indecision; I close the distance in two strides and end it with a clean strike.

}
#if(v_strength >= 3 && v_agility < 3) {
Refusing to be trapped in a cell, I prepare to use my physical leverage.

Looking at the damaged gargoyle pen, I grab an eight-foot iron bar from the bent lattice, brace my boots, and wrench it free from the masonry with a sharp heave. The heavy iron makes a devastating bludgeon.

I step into the lead griffin's rush, swinging the iron bar with full torque. The blow connects with a skull-cracking thud, driving the beast into the stones. The second charges; I swing again, the momentum launching it headfirst into the opposite wall. Dropping low, I sweep the bar across the front legs of the remaining two, shattering bone and bringing them down together. Two clean thrusts with the iron point finish the match.

}
#if(v_agility < 3 && v_strength < 3) {
I rush the nearest griffin, driving my dagger into its neck before it sets its stance. The remaining three spread their wings, hissing and advancing together. One lunges with its beak; I slip the strike and blind it with a thrust, but attempt to retreat too late. A talon rakes my back from the blind spot, and the third griffin drives its beak through my chest. Darkness rushes in.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic) if (v_agility >= 3 || v_strength >= 3)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_agility < 3 && v_strength < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_agility < 3 && v_strength < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_agility < 3 && v_strength < 3)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Slowly
TEXT:

I rise slowly, feigning a clumsy stagger as if wounded. The giant rats halt, sniffing the air, unsure whether to press the attack or retreat.

Their hesitation is fatal. The griffins spot the clustering rodents and dive across the gallery. The lead raptor pounces onto a rat, snapping its spine with a single beak crunch. The rest of the pack panics, but the griffins run them down with ease. Within moments, all four beasts are pinned to the flags, tearing hungrily into their prey and ignoring me entirely.

They are separated and focused on feeding. I can take them down quietly one by one.

As I close the distance on the nearest griffin, dagger in hand, a ridiculous impulse flares up. It rests on its belly, engrossed in its meal, completely unbothered by my approach. A childhood fantasy of griffin riders flashes through my head.

No. Idiotic. Suicidal.

I push the thought down, stepping closer. The griffin holds the rat with heavy talons, tearing strips with its beak. Still oblivious.

A quick ride wouldn't hurt. When will I ever have another chance? If it thrashes, I can jump off and finish it with the dagger.

choice("Try riding the griffin", B2-Ch02a-Rider, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Rider)
choice("Kill the griffin directly", B2-Ch02a-Directly, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Directly)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Rider
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch2_griffin,1) if (v_toughness >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_toughness >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_toughness < 3)
achievement("Griffin rider",v_ac_b2_ch2_griffin)
#if(v_toughness >= 3) {
Here goes nothing.

I snatch a handful of feathery ruff and vault onto its back, locking my thighs around its flank. The griffin bolts upright with an outraged screech, wings thrashing violently to dislodge me. I grip the ruff and stay seated, but the beast snaps its neck backward, beak stabbing for my face. I throw myself off to evade the strike, losing my footing and crashing onto the flags.

The griffin pounces instantly, rear claws digging into my chest. The talons pierce the linen and skin, but dense muscle stops them cold. I drive my dagger into its hock, forcing the claws to retract, roll clear, and lunge forward, slicing its throat wide open.

Well. I rode a griffin. And I will never do it again.

A second griffin spots the struggle and approaches with wings spread. I meet the charge, driving my enchanted blade straight into its heart, matching the anatomical diagrams from my notebook with absolute precision. The beast drops limp.

The remaining two are still feeding, oblivious to the carnage behind them. I dispatch the third with a clean thrust to the spine, then turn on the fourth, burying my blade in its chest before it can scramble upright.

}
#if(v_toughness < 3) {
Here goes nothing.

I snatch a handful of feathery ruff and vault onto its back, locking my thighs around its flank. The griffin bolts upright with an outraged screech, wings thrashing violently to dislodge me. I grip the ruff and stay seated, but the beast snaps its neck backward, beak stabbing for my face. I throw myself off to evade the strike, losing my footing and crashing onto the flags.

The griffin pounces instantly, driving its massive rear talons through my ribs into my heart. Death is instantaneous.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic) if (v_toughness >= 3)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_toughness < 3)
