choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_toughness < 3)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Directly
TEXT:

On second thought, survival takes priority over childhood fantasies.

I suppress the impulse and drive my dagger into the griffin's throat as it lowers its beak to feed, severing the vocal cords to stifle any death screech.

A second griffin nearby looks up from its kill and stalks toward me. I wait until it enters range, then step into its guard and plant the enchanted blade cleanly through its heart, matching the anatomy diagrams from my notes. It collapses without a sound.

The remaining two are still feeding down the corridor. I slip behind the third, executing it with a thrust to the base of the skull, then pivot to the fourth, driving my dagger through its ribs before it can scramble upright.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Acrobatic
TEXT:

Down the gallery, Leila is already finishing her fight. The harpies lie in mangled heaps of feathers, leaving two dire wolves and a single sabre-toothed cat.

As I close the distance, she vaults in a clean, low cartwheel over the lead wolf, landing with both wooden sandals driving the second wolf's head into the flagstones with a sickening crack. In the same motion, she draws both belt daggers and whips them underhand; one blade buries itself between the eyes of the remaining wolf, the other into the temple of the tiger. Both predators drop limp.

"Griffins and gargoyles are down," I tell her as she retrieves her daggers and wipes the steel along a wolf's flank. "Some rats scattered, but they aren't a threat. We are clear here."

Not yet, Leila writes. We need to know who released the pens.

"If they were in this gallery, we would have spotted them," I say. "Only one route leads in from the lake pool. Whoever tripped the gates must be further down the line."

Agreed, she writes. Move.

We advance to the end of the gallery, daggers drawn, and enter a narrow stone passage. Near the entrance, two rough timber chairs lie overturned on the gravel, beside a bowl of warm, spilled stew.

"Looks like the handlers made a run for it," I say.

Likely, Leila writes. Expect an ambush at the tunnel mouth. Ready your steel.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Trolls, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Trolls)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Leave
TEXT:

"Leave the beasts," I say. "Finding an exit takes priority."

Agreed, Leila writes, letting the stone drop from her fingers. Let's move.

We hurry down the gallery and enter a narrow stone corridor beyond the pens, daggers ready. Near the entrance, two rough timber chairs lie overturned on the gravel, beside a bowl of warm, spilled stew.

"We've been spotted," I say.

Yes, Leila writes. Expect an ambush at the tunnel mouth. Ready your steel.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Trolls, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Trolls)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Trolls
TEXT:

The moment we clear the tunnel mouth, six iron-tipped lances level at our chests.

The wielders are broad, gray-skinned brutes with hooked noses, jutting yellow tusks, and wild, unkempt manes: trolls.

They lack the cunning of goblins, but easily outmatch ogres in intellect. Worse is their regenerative biology—flesh knits and bone mends in seconds unless severed cleanly or cauterized with fire.

"Drop weapons!" the lead troll barks in harsh, broken Common.

Leila and I exchange a tense look, knuckles tightening on our hilts.

"Drop steel now, or die where you stand!" the troll snarls, taking a heavy stride forward.

A voice echoes from the darkness behind them:

"Somehow, I knew you would be the source of all this noise."

"Flower?" I call out.

A ribbon of compressed, high-pressure water whips out of the shadows. The liquid blade snaps across the corridor in a single blinding horizontal arc, shearing through flesh and bone. All six troll heads tumble to the gravel in unison, followed by the heavy thud of their collapsing bodies.

Flower steps into the cobalt light, her eyes glowing brilliant, electric blue.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch02a-Ending, v_current_scene = B2-Ch02a-Ending)


ID: B2-Ch02a-Ending
TEXT:

"Flower is asleep," Illuna says coolly.

She casts a sharp, assessing glance over Leila before fixing her gaze on me.

"I see you acquired a companion."

"Met her in the division cells," I say. "A fellow gladiator."

Leila steps forward, extending a hand while glowing blue script forms in the air:

I am Leila. Well met.

"Charmed," Illuna says in her flat, aristocratic drone, completely unfazed by the floating text.

Without taking the offered hand, she turns on her heel and strides down the corridor.

"Why are you lingering?" she calls over her shoulder. "Keep pace."

"Where are we heading?" I ask, catching up.

"There is an associate of mine you should meet," Illuna says. "His objectives align remarkably well with your own."

"What associate? What are you even doing down here?"

"Really, Barry, I assumed that much was obvious," Illuna says, glancing back with dry amusement. "We came to sabotage the arena."

